"A plane passes through $(0,0,0)$ with normal $(0,0,1)$. What is its equation?","options":["$x=0$","$z=0$","$y=0$","$x+y+z=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"585d34e8ea087357fa6e74e0","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $d$ for the plane $2x+y-z=d$ passing through $(1,3,2)$?","options":["$6$","$0$","$3$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d0e3208c3fec24c3a7d8a4c0","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens if the coefficient vector $(a,b,c)$ is zero in $ax+by+cz=d$?","options":["It always gives the $xy$-plane.","It gives a unique point.","It gives a normal vector of length one, which fixes the plane.","The equation no longer determines an ordinary plane."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"88cf8d61758095c1f6bb429a","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Write the point-normal equation for the plane through $(1,2,3)$ with normal $(2,-1,4)$.","options":["$2x-y+4z=0$","$(x-2)+2(y+1)+3(z-4)=0$","$2(x-1)-(y-2)+4(z-3)=0$","$2(x+1)-y+2+4(z+3)=0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ffabe6c94d19aa649e23ef9f","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Rewrite $2(x-1)-(y-2)+4(z-3)=0$ as $ax+by+cz=d$.","options":["$2x+y+4z=12$","$x-2y+4z=12$","$2x-y+4z=0$","$2x-y+4z=12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"26ed2ffccc1cde935fb6af6e","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which point lies on the plane $2x-y+z=3$?","options":["$(2,0,0)$","$(1,1,1)$","$(1,0,1)$","$(0,0,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"715b5c3b5f66521aa0883ed0","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the point-normal equation describe an infinite set of points rather than just the fixed point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$?","options":["Many displacement vectors can be perpendicular to the same normal vector.","Only the zero displacement is perpendicular to a normal vector.","The fixed point is not actually on the plane.","The equation forces $x=x_0$, $y=y_0$, and $z=z_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9903773e94134525ed4347d4","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the equation $ax+by+cz=d$ determine the normal direction but not a unique normal vector?","options":["The normal vector is unique only when $d=0$.","Any vector lying in the plane can serve as a normal vector.","Any vector as long as $(a,b,c)$ is also a normal vector.","Any nonzero multiple of $(a,b,c)$ is also a normal vector."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"528556983c48fd1a1f9498ee","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch14_point_normal_equation_of_a_plane_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which vector lies in the plane direction space of $2x-y+z=3$?","options":["$(1,3,1)$","$(0,0,3)$","$(2,-1,1)$","$(1,0,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"721faf2e7ee0e12bbb5ff868","time_limit":40}]}}